        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-multivariate-normal-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC and let $d\\ge1$ be finite. For $m\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and a real symmetric positive semidefinite matrix $\\Sigma$, a Borel probability law is denoted $N_d(m,\\Sigma)$ when a vector X with that law satisfies $u\\cdot X\\sim N(u\\cdot m,u^T\\Sigma u)$ for every $u\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Such a law exists, has mean m and covariance Sigma, and can be realized as $m+\\Sigma^{1/2}Z$ with independent standard-normal coordinates. Singular Sigma is allowed. Uniqueness will be proved in the following characteristic-function lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-finite-dimensional-orthogonal-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $W$ is a subspace of a finite-dimensional real or complex inner product space $V$, then",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $n,m\\ge1$. Every continuous map\n$f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ is Borel measurable in\nthe sense of [[def-borel-and-lebesgue-measurable-function-on-rn]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $k=0$, the first column is nonzero almost surely: the event $G_1=0$ is contained in the event that its first coordinate is zero, which has probability zero because a standard normal has a density. For $1\\le k<p$, suppose the first $k$ columns are linearly independent. For each fixed such tuple $v_1,\\ldots,v_k$, their span $V$ is proper; by [F3] choose $a\\ne0$ with $a\\perp V$. The next column is independent of this tuple by [F1], and [F2] gives $a^TG_{k+1}\\sim N(0,\\|a\\|^2)$, so $\\mathbb P(G_{k+1}\\in V)=0$ because that event is contained in $\\{a^TG_{k+1}=0\\}$. The set of dependent $(k+1)$-tuples is Borel, since dependence is the simultaneous vanishing of its $(k+1)\\times(k+1)$ minors and [F5] makes these determinant maps Borel. Integrating these zero-probability sections by [F1] and [F4], and adding the null set of dependent first $k$-tuples, shows that the first $k+1$ columns are linearly independent almost surely.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Applying step 1.1 successively through $k=p-1$ shows that the square matrix $B=[G_1|\\cdots|G_p]$ is invertible almost surely. For any nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^p$, the first $p$ coordinates of $G^Tv$ are $B^Tv\\ne0$, so $v^TGG^Tv=\\|G^Tv\\|^2>0$. Thus $GG^T$ is positive definite almost surely.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
