    "lem-standard-wishart-independent-normal-quadratic-form-is-f": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-wishart-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $p,m\\ge1$ be integers and let $\\Sigma$ be a real symmetric positive-semidefinite $p\\times p$ matrix, in the sense of [[def-multivariate-normal-law]]. Give $\\mathrm{Sym}_p$, the space of real symmetric $p\\times p$ matrices, its Euclidean Borel sigma-algebra by identifying a matrix with its upper-triangular entries. The finite product measure $N_p(0,\\Sigma)^{\\otimes m}$ exists because each factor is a probability measure and hence sigma-finite. Define the **Wishart law** $W_p(\\Sigma,m)$ as the pushforward of this product measure by the continuous map $(z_1,\\ldots,z_m)\\mapsto\\sum_{j=1}^m z_jz_j^T$. Equivalently, it is the law of $\\sum_{j=1}^m Z_jZ_j^T$ for independent $Z_j\\sim N_p(0,\\Sigma)$.\n\nHere $\\Sigma$ is the scale matrix and the positive integer $m$ is the degrees of freedom. This definition allows a singular Wishart law when $m<p$ or $\\Sigma$ is singular; no Lebesgue density is included in the convention. The usual nonsingular density when $m\\ge p$ is not needed below.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-standard-normal-matrix-has-full-row-rank-almost-surely",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $1\\le p\\le m$ and let $G=[G_1|\\cdots|G_m]$ be a $p\\times m$ matrix whose columns are independent $N_p(0,I_p)$ random vectors. Then the first $p$ columns are linearly independent almost surely. Consequently $GG^T$ is positive definite almost surely.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-orthogonal-transform-preserves-standard-normal-product-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $n\\ge1$, let $Z\\sim N_n(0,I_n)$ have independent standard-normal coordinates, and let $U\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$ satisfy $U^TU=I_n$. Then $UZ$ also has independent $N(0,1)$ coordinates. Here $I_n$ is the identity matrix and $U$ is orthogonal.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-orthogonal-projection-has-independent-chi-square-sums",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $n\\ge1$, let $Z\\sim N_n(0,I_n)$, and let $P\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$ be an orthogonal projection of rank $r$, where $0\\le r\\le n$. Then the random vectors $PZ$ and $(I_n-P)Z$ are independent, and\n$$\n\\|PZ\\|^2\\sim\\chi_r^2,\\qquad \\|(I_n-P)Z\\|^2\\sim\\chi_{n-r}^2.\n$$\nThe two squared norms are independent. The cases $r=0$ and $r=n$ are included, with $\\chi_0^2=\\delta_0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-chi-square-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For an integer $k\\ge1$, let $G=(G_1,\\ldots,G_k)$ have law $N_k(0,I_k)$, so its coordinates are independent $N(0,1)$ random variables. The **chi-square law with $k$ degrees of freedom**, denoted $\\chi_k^2$, is the law of\n$$\nQ_k=\\sum_{j=1}^k G_j^2=\\|G\\|^2.\n$$\nFor $k=0$, define $\\chi_0^2=\\delta_0$, the point mass at zero. This zero-degree law records a rank-zero projection only; it is not used as a denominator in a Student or Fisher ratio. The multivariate-normal characteristic-function theorem makes the definition independent of the chosen realization of $G$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-student-t-and-f-laws",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For a positive integer $k$, take the product probability law of an independent standard normal $G$ and an independent $Q_k\\sim\\chi_k^2$. The **Student t law with $k$ degrees of freedom**, denoted $t_k$, is the law of\n$$T_k=\\begin{cases} \\displaystyle\\frac{G}{\\sqrt{Q_k/k}},&Q_k>0,\\\\ 0,&Q_k\\le0. \\end{cases}$$\nFor positive integers $r,s$, take independent $Q_r\\sim\\chi_r^2$ and $Q_s\\sim\\chi_s^2$. The **Fisher F law with degrees $(r,s)$**, denoted $F_{r,s}$, is the law of\n$$F_{r,s}=\\begin{cases} \\displaystyle\\frac{Q_r/r}{Q_s/s},&Q_s>0,\\\\ 0,&Q_s\\le0. \\end{cases}$$\nThe displayed piecewise maps are defined on all of $\\mathbb R^2$ and are Borel measurable: each quotient is continuous on its Borel positive-denominator region, and the value on the complementary Borel nonpositive-denominator region is constant. The positive-degree chi-square lemma proves that the denominators are positive almost surely, so these versions agree almost surely with the usual quotients. Zero degrees are excluded from both definitions.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-positive-df-chi-square-has-density-and-regular-cdf",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For every integer $k\\ge1$, the law $\\chi_k^2$ has density\n$$\nf_k(x)=c_k x^{k/2-1}e^{-x/2}\\mathbf 1_{\\{x>0\\}},\\qquad c_k=\\frac{\\sigma_{k-1}(S^{k-1})}{2(2\\pi)^{k/2}}>0,\n$$\nwhere $\\sigma_{k-1}$ is the polar surface measure on the unit sphere in $\\mathbb R^k$. Consequently, $Q_k>0$ almost surely, and its cumulative distribution function is continuous on $\\mathbb R$ and strictly increasing on $(0,\\infty)$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-independent-random-elements",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ be random elements on a\ncommon probability space. For each $i$, write\n\n$$\\sigma(X_i):=\\{X_i^{-1}(B):B\\in\\Sigma_i\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal F.$$\n\nThe family $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ is **independent** when the sigma-algebras\n$(\\sigma(X_i))_{i\\in I}$ are independent in the sense of\n[[def-independent-sigma-algebras-and-events]].\n\nWhen $\\Sigma_i$ is a Borel sigma-algebra, this agrees with the notation\n$\\sigma(X_i)$ from [[def-sigma-algebra-generated-by-a-function]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-grouping-independent-sigma-algebras",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal F_i)_{i\\in I}$ be an independent family of sigma-algebras on a\nprobability space, and let $J_0,\\dots,J_{m-1}\\subseteq I$ be pairwise disjoint\nindex sets. For each $r<m$, define\n\n$$\\mathcal G_r:=\\sigma\\left(\\bigcup_{i\\in J_r}\\mathcal F_i\\right).$$\n\nThen the sigma-algebras $\\mathcal G_0,\\dots,\\mathcal G_{m-1}$ are independent.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-measurable-functions-preserve-independence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be an independent family of random elements\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$. For each $i$, let\n$g_i:(S_i,\\Sigma_i)\\to(T_i,\\mathcal T_i)$ be measurable. Then the family\n$(g_i\\circ X_i)_{i\\in I}$ is independent.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-independent-random-elements-have-product-joint-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, and let\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ for $i<n$ be independent\nrandom elements. Define\n\n$$X=(X_0,\\dots,X_{n-1}):\\Omega\\to\\prod_{i<n}S_i.$$\n\nThen $X$ is a random element of\n$\\left(\\prod_{i<n}S_i,\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\Sigma_i\\right)$, and its law is the\nfinite product of the marginal laws:\n\n$$\\mathbb P_X=\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathbb P_{X_i}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-orthogonal-projection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $W$ be a subspace of a finite-dimensional inner product space $V$. The orthogonal-decomposition theorem ([[thm-finite-dimensional-orthogonal-decomposition]]) gives\n\n$$V=W\\oplus W^\\perp$$\n\nand associates to every $v\\in V$ unique vectors $w\\in W$ and $z\\in W^\\perp$ with $v=w+z$. The **orthogonal projection onto $W$** is the function\n\n$$P_W:V\\longrightarrow W,\\qquad P_Wv=w.$$\n\nEquivalently, $P_Wv$ is the unique vector of $W$ such that $v-P_Wv\\in W^\\perp$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-self-adjoint-idempotents-are-exactly-orthogonal-projections",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "An endomorphism $P$ of a finite-dimensional inner product space is the orthogonal projection onto some subspace if and only if\n\n$$P^2=P\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad P^*=P.$$\n\nIn that case it is the orthogonal projection onto $\\operatorname{im}P$, along $\\ker P=(\\operatorname{im}P)^\\perp$. The cases $P=0$ and $P=I$ are included.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-finite-dimensional-orthogonal-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $W$ is a subspace of a finite-dimensional real or complex inner product space $V$, then\n\n$$V=W\\oplus W^\\perp.$$\n\nThus every $v\\in V$ has unique vectors $w\\in W$ and $z\\in W^\\perp$ with $v=w+z$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "cor-finite-dimensional-inner-product-spaces-have-orthonormal-bases",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every finite-dimensional real or complex inner product space has an orthonormal basis. In dimension zero, this is the empty basis.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $n,m\\ge1$. Every continuous map\n$f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ is Borel measurable in\nthe sense of [[def-borel-and-lebesgue-measurable-function-on-rn]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-ac-supplies-sequential-choices-for-probability-constructions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Then countable choice holds. Moreover, if $R\\subseteq X\\times X$ is serial on a nonempty set X and $a\\in X$, there is a sequence $(x_n)_{n\\ge0}$ with $x_0=a$ and $x_n R x_{n+1}$ for every n. Thus the countable and dependent choices required by the probability-product suppliers are available under AC.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], realize $W=GG^T$ with $G=[g_1|\\cdots|g_m]$ and independent $g_j\\sim N_p(0,I_p)$. For any square matrix $A$, extend inversion by setting $J(A)=A^{-1}$ when $\\det A\\ne0$ and $J(A)=0$ otherwise. This is Borel: the invertible matrices form an open set, inversion is continuous there, and the complementary branch is constant; [F11] supplies Euclidean Borel measurability. Thus $J(GG^T)$ is a Borel matrix-valued random element and agrees with $W^{-1}$ whenever $GG^T$ is invertible. Since $m\\ge p$, [F2] makes $G$ full row rank almost surely; hence for nonzero $v$, $v^TWv=\\|G^Tv\\|^2>0$ and $W$ is invertible almost surely.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F11",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For $p\\ge2$, write the first row of $G$ as $g$ and the remaining $p-1$ rows as $G_2$. The event $E_2=\\{\\det(G_2G_2^T)\\ne0\\}$ is Borel. Define $C^\\dagger=(G_2G_2^T)^{-1}$ on $E_2$ and $C^\\dagger=0$ on $E_2^c$, and put $P=G_2^TC^\\dagger G_2$; this is Borel by the same piecewise-inversion argument as in step 1.1. By [F2], $E_2$ has probability one. On $E_2$, direct multiplication gives $P^T=P$ and $P^2=P$, and its image is the row space of $G_2$, of dimension $p-1$; [F9] identifies it as an orthogonal projection. For $p=1$, use the empty-row convention $G_2=0$, $P=0$. In either case define the Borel scalar $Q=g(I_m-P)g^T$ on the whole matrix space. On the full-probability event that $G$ has full row rank, the block matrix $W=GG^T=\\begin{pmatrix}gg^T&gG_2^T\\\\G_2g^T&C\\end{pmatrix}$ has invertible lower block $C$ when $p\\ge2$, and solving $Wx=e_1$ gives $e_1^TW^{-1}e_1=(gg^T-gG_2^TC^{-1}G_2g^T)^{-1}$. Thus for $p\\ge2$ this $Q$ equals $(e_1^TW^{-1}e_1)^{-1}$ on the full-probability event. For $p=1$, $W=gg^T$ and the same identity reads $Q=gg^T=(e_1^TW^{-1}e_1)^{-1}$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F9",
            "F11",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The entries of $G$ are independent standard normals, so its first row $g$ is independent of the remaining rows $G_2$ by [F7]. For each fixed full-row-rank value of $G_2$, $I_m-P$ is an orthogonal projection of rank $m-p+1$. Therefore [F4] gives the section law $Q\\sim\\chi^2_{m-p+1}$ for every such $G_2$; the $p=1$ case follows from [F5]. The matrix rank event is Borel because it is determined by continuous minors, and the extended map $P(G_2)$ is Borel by step 1.2. The exceptional set has probability zero by [F2]. Applying the product-law identity [F8] to these Borel sections shows that the unconditional law of the Borel extension $Q=g(I_m-P)g^T$ is also $\\chi^2_{m-p+1}$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F11",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For any fixed unit vector $u\\in\\mathbb R^p$, [F10] supplies an orthogonal matrix $U$ with $Uu=e_1$. By [F3] and [F7], applying $U$ to each independent column preserves their joint standard-normal product law; hence $UWU^T$ has the same law as $W$. Let $h(t)=t^{-1}$ for $t>0$ and $h(t)=0$ otherwise. Since $J(W)=W^{-1}$ almost surely by step 1.1 and $W\\succ0$ almost surely, $h(u^TJ(W)u)$ has the same law as $Q$ in step 2.1 for every unit $u$, using the orthogonal invariance just proved. Set $U_Z=Z/\\|Z\\|$ when $Z\\ne0$ and let $U_Z=e_1$ when $Z=0$. The map $z\\mapsto U_z$ is Borel, and $Q_Z:=h(U_Z^TJ(W)U_Z)$ is Borel because $J$ and $h$ are Borel; $Z=0$ is null by [F5]–[F6]. For Borel sets $A,B$, independence and the product law give\n$$\\mathbb P(\\|Z\\|^2\\in A,Q_Z\\in B)=\\int_{\\{z:\\|z\\|^2\\in A\\}}\\mathbb P_W\\bigl(h(u_z^TJ(W)u_z)\\in B\\bigr)\\,d\\mathbb P_Z(z),$$\nwhere $u_z=z/\\|z\\|$ for $z\\ne0$ and $u_0=e_1$. The inner probability is the same for every unit $u_z$, so this factors as $\\mathbb P(\\|Z\\|^2\\in A)\\mathbb P(Q\\in B)$. Thus $Q_Z$ is independent of $\\|Z\\|^2$ and has the same $\\chi^2_{m-p+1}$ law as $Q$ in step 2.1.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F10",
            "F11",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "By [F5], $\\|Z\\|^2\\sim\\chi_p^2$ and it is independent of $Q_Z\\sim\\chi^2_{m-p+1}$ by step 3.1. Therefore $\\frac{m-p+1}{p}Z^TW^{-1}Z=\\frac{\\|Z\\|^2/p}{Q_Z/(m-p+1)}$ has law $F_{p,m-p+1}$ by the defining Fisher ratio; its denominator is positive almost surely by [F6].",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
