    "thm-covariance-likelihood-ratio-asymptotic-chi-square": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "lem-ac-supplies-sequential-choices-for-probability-constructions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Then countable choice holds. Moreover, if $R\\subseteq X\\times X$ is serial on a nonempty set X and $a\\in X$, there is a sequence $(x_n)_{n\\ge0}$ with $x_0=a$ and $x_n R x_{n+1}$ for every n. Thus the countable and dependent choices required by the probability-product suppliers are available under AC.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-multivariate-normal-sample-model-and-statistics",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Fix integers $n\\ge2$ and $p\\ge1$. Let $\\mathcal S_{++}^p$ be the set of real symmetric $p\\times p$ matrices $\\Sigma$ such that $v^T\\Sigma v>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^p$. The iid multivariate-normal sampling experiment has data space $\\mathcal X=(\\mathbb R^p)^n$ with sigma-algebra $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^p)^{\\otimes n}$, parameter set $\\Theta=\\mathbb R^p\\times\\mathcal S_{++}^p$, and sampling law $P_{\\mu,\\Sigma}=N_p(\\mu,\\Sigma)^{\\otimes n}$. The finite product is a probability measure by [[def-probability-measure]] and [[thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique]]. The coordinate projection $X_i$ is the complete vector observation for sampling unit $i$; its $p$ coordinates need not be independent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-multivariate-normal-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC and let $d\\ge1$ be finite. For $m\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and a real symmetric positive semidefinite matrix $\\Sigma$, a Borel probability law is denoted $N_d(m,\\Sigma)$ when a vector X with that law satisfies $u\\cdot X\\sim N(u\\cdot m,u^T\\Sigma u)$ for every $u\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Such a law exists, has mean m and covariance Sigma, and can be realized as $m+\\Sigma^{1/2}Z$ with independent standard-normal coordinates. Singular Sigma is allowed. Uniqueness will be proved in the following characteristic-function lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Define $\\gamma(E)=\\int_E e^{-x^2/2}/\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,dx$ for Borel E in $\\mathbb R$. By [[lem-normal-density-has-total-mass-one]] and [[thm-indefinite-integral-of-a-nonnegative-function-is-a-measure]], gamma is a probability measure; denote it $N(0,1)$. For $m\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\sigma\\ge0$, define $N(m,\\sigma^2)$ as the law of $x\\mapsto m+\\sigma x$ on $(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B,\\gamma)$. This affine map is continuous: for $\\sigma$>0 choose $\\delta$=$\\varepsilon$/$\\sigma$, and for $\\sigma$=0 it is constant. Its inverse images of opens are open, so it is Borel measurable. [[lem-law-of-a-random-element-is-a-probability-measure]] makes its pushforward a probability. When $\\sigma$=0, the preimage of E is all of R if m belongs to E and empty otherwise, so $N(m,0)=\\delta_m$ in [[def-dirac-measure]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-independent-random-elements",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ be random elements on a\ncommon probability space. For each $i$, write",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-independent-random-elements-have-product-joint-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, and let\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ for $i<n$ be independent\nrandom elements. Define",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-lebesgue-product-measure-agrees-with-euclidean-lebesgue-on-borel-sets",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$m,n \\ge 1$. Under the identification\n$$\\mathbb R^{m+n}=\\mathbb R^m \\times \\mathbb R^n,$$\nthe product measure $\\lambda_m \\times \\lambda_n$ and the Euclidean Lebesgue\nmeasure $\\lambda_{m+n}$ agree on every Borel subset of $\\mathbb R^{m+n}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-borel-functions-via-radon-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $m\\ge1$, let U,V be open subsets of $\\mathbb R^m$, and let $T:U\\to V$ be a $C^1$ diffeomorphism. For every nonnegative Borel $h:V\\to[0,\\infty]$, $\\int_V h(y)\\,dy=\\int_U h(T(x))|\\det DT(x)|\\,dx$, with equality in $[0,\\infty]$ and $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. This statement concerns Borel h; no completed-measurable substitution is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-standard-normal-matrix-has-full-row-rank-almost-surely",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $1\\le p\\le m$ and let $G=[G_1|\\cdots|G_m]$ be a $p\\times m$ matrix whose columns are independent $N_p(0,I_p)$ random vectors. Then the first $p$ columns are linearly independent almost surely. Consequently $GG^T$ is positive definite almost surely.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-multivariate-iid-central-limit-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC and let $d\\ge1$ be a finite integer. Let $(X_k)_{k\\ge1}$ be iid $\\mathbb R^d$-valued random vectors with $\\mathbb E\\|X_1\\|^2<\\infty$, mean m and covariance Sigma. Then $$n^{-1/2}\\sum_{k=1}^n(X_k-m)\\Rightarrow N_d(0,\\Sigma).$$ The covariance may be singular.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-vector-stochastic-order-calculus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let all random vectors with index $n$ be defined on a common probability space for that $n$; the spaces may vary with $n$. In any fixed finite-dimensional Euclidean space, the following rules hold:",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-op-and-little-op-stochastic-bounds",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For each positive integer $n$, let $(\\Omega_n,\\mathcal A_n,P_n)$ be a probability space and let $X_n:\\Omega_n\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be measurable into the Borel sigma-algebra generated by the Euclidean open sets, where $d\\ge1$ is fixed and $\\mathbb R^d$ has the Euclidean norm $\\|\\cdot\\|_2$ ([[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]], [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]], [[def-probability-measure]]). The spaces and measures may depend on $n$; no common probability space is required.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-vector-slutsky",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d,k,m\\ge1$ be fixed. For each $n$, let $X_n\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and $Y_n\\in\\mathbb R^k$ be random vectors on one probability space; the probability space may vary with $n$. Let $X\\in\\mathbb R^d$ be a random vector on any probability space, and let $c\\in\\mathbb R^k$ be constant. If $X_n\\Rightarrow X$ and $Y_n-c=o_P(1)$, then\n$$ (X_n,Y_n)\\Rightarrow(X,c). $$\nConsequently, for every continuous $h:\\mathbb R^{d+k}\\to\\mathbb R^m$, $h(X_n,Y_n)\\Rightarrow h(X,c)$. No independence between $X_n$ and $Y_n$ is required.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-continuous-mapping-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let S,T be metric spaces, $g:S\\to T$ measurable, and $\\mu_n\\Rightarrow\\mu$. If the discontinuity set $D_g$ is $\\mu$-null, then $g_*\\mu_n\\Rightarrow g_*\\mu$. Consequently $X_n\\Rightarrow X$ implies $g(X_n)\\Rightarrow g(X)$ whenever $P_X(D_g)=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-chi-square-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For an integer $k\\ge1$, let $G=(G_1,\\ldots,G_k)$ have law $N_k(0,I_k)$, so its coordinates are independent $N(0,1)$ random variables. The **chi-square law with $k$ degrees of freedom**, denoted $\\chi_k^2$, is the law of\n$$\nQ_k=\\sum_{j=1}^k G_j^2=\\|G\\|^2.\n$$\nFor $k=0$, define $\\chi_0^2=\\delta_0$, the point mass at zero. This zero-degree law records a rank-zero projection only; it is not used as a denominator in a Student or Fisher ratio. The multivariate-normal characteristic-function theorem makes the definition independent of the chosen realization of $G$.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "def-wald-score-likelihood-ratio-statistics",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC for the supplied density representatives in\n[[def-score-and-fisher-information]] and [[def-axiom-of-choice]]. Let\n$X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be iid from a dominated parametric model on an open\n$\\Theta\\subset\\mathbb R^d$, and let $\\ell_n(\\theta)=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\log\np_\\theta(X_i)$. For any nested null $\\Theta_0\\subseteq\\Theta$ for which the\nmaxima exist, the **likelihood-ratio statistic** is\n$$L_n=2\\left\\{\\sup_{\\theta\\in\\Theta}\\ell_n(\\theta)-\\sup_{\\theta\\in\\Theta_0}\\ell_n(\\theta)\\right\\}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space and let $T:V\\to V$ be\nself-adjoint. Then $V$ has an orthonormal basis consisting of eigenvectors of\n$T$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "thm-non-negative-square-root-exists-and-is-unique",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real or complex inner product space and let\n$T:V\\to V$ be non-negative. Then there exists a unique non-negative operator\n$R:V\\to V$ such that",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "def-dominated-statistical-experiment",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$\nbe a statistical experiment ([[def-statistical-experiment]]). It is\n**dominated** by a supplied positive measure $\\mu$ on\n$(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ when $\\mu$ is sigma-finite\n([[def-finite-sigma-finite-and-semifinite-measures]]) and\n$P_\\theta\\ll\\mu$ for every $\\theta\\in\\Theta$\n([[def-absolutely-continuous-with-respect-to-a-positive-measure]]).\nThe dominating measure is part of the specified setup. This condition adds no\nmeasurable structure on $\\Theta$ and no measurability of\n$\\theta\\mapsto P_\\theta$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "prop-lebesgue-measure-is-sigma-finite-and-finite-on-bounded-sets",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\ge 1$ and **assume the Axiom of Countable Choice**\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Then:",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "def-likelihood-density",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$\nbe dominated by the fixed sigma-finite positive measure $\\mu$. For each\n$\\theta$, the Radon–Nikodym theorem gives the almost-everywhere class\n$dP_\\theta/d\\mu$; a sigma-finite exhaustion for $\\mu$ is also finite for the\nprobability measure $P_\\theta$. Under AC, select simultaneously a\nfinite-valued measurable nonnegative representative $p_\\theta$ from each\nclass. The nonnegative version exists by\n[[lem-radon-nikodym-density-of-a-finite-positive-measure-has-a-nonnegative-representative]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F15",
          "source": "cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $n,m\\ge1$. Every continuous map\n$f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ is Borel measurable in\nthe sense of [[def-borel-and-lebesgue-measurable-function-on-rn]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F2] and [F3], one observation has density $\\phi_{\\mu,\\Sigma}(x)=(2\\pi)^{-p/2}(\\det\\Sigma)^{-1/2}\\exp[-(x-\\mu)^T\\Sigma^{-1}(x-\\mu)/2]$: the independent-coordinate product density follows from [F4], its continuous Gaussian formula is Borel by [F15], and the invertible affine map $z\\mapsto\\mu+\\Sigma^{1/2}z$ has determinant $|\\det\\Sigma^{1/2}|=(\\det\\Sigma)^{1/2}$, so [F5] gives the displayed density. Independence in [F1] then makes the sample density its product over $i=1,\\ldots,n$. The finite-dimensional Lebesgue measure is sigma-finite by [F13], so the product law is dominated by it and the displayed function is a likelihood density by [F14].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F13",
            "F14",
            "F15",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Under $H_0$, the positive root $\\Sigma_0^{1/2}$ is invertible by [F12], so define $Y_i=\\Sigma_0^{-1/2}(X_i-\\mu)$. By [F1]–[F2] these are iid $N_p(0,I_p)$. Write $C_n=n^{-1}\\sum_iY_iY_i^T$ and $A_n=\\Sigma_0^{-1/2}M\\Sigma_0^{-1/2}=C_n-\\bar Y\\bar Y^T$. For a symmetric matrix, let $\\operatorname{vech}$ list its upper-triangular entries in a fixed order; put $V_i=\\operatorname{vech}(Y_iY_i^T-I_p)$. These vectors have mean zero and diagonal covariance: diagonal coordinates have variance $2$, off-diagonal coordinates variance $1$, and distinct coordinates have covariance zero. Indeed, independence and symmetry give zero for any product with an odd coordinate power; integration by parts against [F3]'s density gives $E Z^2=1$ and $E Z^4=3$ (using $\\phi'(z)=-z\\phi(z)$ and vanishing polynomial-times-Gaussian boundary terms), while independence factors mixed even products. These finite moments make $V_i$ square-integrable, so [F7] yields $\\sqrt n\\operatorname{vech}(C_n-I_p)\\Rightarrow N(0,D)$ with those diagonal variances. Also $\\sqrt n\\bar Y=O_P(1)$ by [F7]–[F8], whence $\\sqrt n\\bar Y\\bar Y^T=n^{-1/2}(\\sqrt n\\bar Y)(\\sqrt n\\bar Y)^T=o_P(1)$ by [F8]. Thus $H_n=\\sqrt n(A_n-I_p)$ converges in distribution to a symmetric Gaussian matrix $H$ whose entries on and above the diagonal are independent, with variances $2$ on the diagonal and $1$ off it.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F12",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Taking logarithms of that positive product density gives, up to the parameter-free constant $-np\\log(2\\pi)/2$, $$\\ell_n(\\mu,\\Sigma)=-\\frac n2\\log\\det\\Sigma-\\frac12\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i-\\mu)^T\\Sigma^{-1}(X_i-\\mu).$$ Expanding $X_i-\\mu=(X_i-\\bar X)+(\\bar X-\\mu)$ and using $\\sum_i(X_i-\\bar X)=0$ shows the quadratic sum equals $n(\\bar X-\\mu)^T\\Sigma^{-1}(\\bar X-\\mu)+\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma^{-1}R)$. Since $\\Sigma^{-1}\\succ0$, this is minimized uniquely at $\\mu=\\bar X$ for every fixed $\\Sigma$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "By [F6], $M=R/n\\succ0$ almost surely. The square root $M^{1/2}$ exists by [F12] and is invertible because $M\\succ0$. At $\\mu=\\bar X$, compare the covariance log likelihood at any $\\Sigma\\succ0$ with its value at $M$. Let $B=M^{1/2}\\Sigma^{-1}M^{1/2}\\succ0$. The trace and determinant identities give $$\\ell_n(\\bar X,\\Sigma)-\\ell_n(\\bar X,M)=-\\frac n2\\{\\operatorname{tr}B-\\log\\det B-p\\}.$$ By [F11], the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_p$ of $B$ are positive and the brace is $\\sum_j(\\lambda_j-\\log\\lambda_j-1)\\ge0$, since $x-\\log x-1\\ge0$ for $x>0$ by differentiation and its unique minimum at $1$. Equality holds precisely when all $\\lambda_j=1$, hence $B=I$ and $\\Sigma=M$. Thus the unrestricted MLE is $(\\bar X,M)$; under $H_0$ the maximizing mean remains $\\bar X$ by step 2.1.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "F11",
            "F12",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Substituting the unrestricted and null maxima into [F10], and using $R=nM$, yields $$-2\\log\\Lambda_n=n\\{\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma_0^{-1}M)+\\log\\det\\Sigma_0-\\log\\det M-p\\}.$$ Since $\\det(\\Sigma_0^{-1/2}M\\Sigma_0^{-1/2})=\\det M/\\det\\Sigma_0$, this is the asserted finite-sample identity.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F10",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Since $H_n=O_P(1)$ by [F8], $E_n=A_n-I_p=H_n/\\sqrt n$ has Frobenius norm $\\|E_n\\|_F=O_P(n^{-1/2})$, where $\\|B\\|_F=(\\sum_{i,j}B_{ij}^2)^{1/2}$. On an event whose probability tends to one, every eigenvalue $e_j$ of the symmetric $E_n$ has $|e_j|\\le1/2$. Taylor's theorem on $[-1/2,1/2]$ gives $e-\\log(1+e)=e^2/2+O(|e|^3)$ with a uniform finite remainder constant there. Hence the statistic in step 4.1 equals $$n\\sum_{j=1}^p(e_j-\\log(1+e_j))=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}(H_n^2)+O_P(n\\|E_n\\|_F^3)=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}(H_n^2)+o_P(1),$$ because $p$ is fixed and $n\\|E_n\\|_F^3=O_P(n^{-1/2})$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 4.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "F8",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "The map $H\\mapsto\\tfrac12\\operatorname{tr}(H^2)$ is continuous, so [F8] and step 1.2 give convergence to $\\tfrac12\\operatorname{tr}(H^2)$. For the stated independent entries this is $$\\sum_{i=1}^p(H_{ii}/\\sqrt2)^2+\\sum_{1\\le i<j\\le p}H_{ij}^2,$$ a sum of $p+p(p-1)/2=p(p+1)/2$ independent squared standard normals, which has law $\\chi^2_{p(p+1)/2}$ by [F9]. Step 5.1 and Slutsky [F8] prove the asserted limit. When $p=1$, the off-diagonal sum is empty and the same count gives one degree of freedom; $p$ is fixed throughout, and no finite-sample calibration follows from this limit.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 5.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
