    "thm-hotelling-t2-exact-f-distribution": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-multivariate-normal-sample-mean-scatter-independence-and-wishart",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC and the sample model in [[def-multivariate-normal-sample-model-and-statistics]], with $n\\ge2$, $p\\ge1$, and $\\Sigma\\succ0$. Then\n$$\\sqrt n(\\bar X-\\mu)\\sim N_p(0,\\Sigma),\\qquad \\bar X\\perp S,\\qquad (n-1)S\\sim W_p(\\Sigma,n-1).$$\nIf $n>p$, then $S$ is positive definite almost surely. The Wishart scale is $\\Sigma$ and its degrees of freedom are $n-1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-wishart-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $p,m\\ge1$ be integers and let $\\Sigma$ be a real symmetric positive-semidefinite $p\\times p$ matrix, in the sense of [[def-multivariate-normal-law]]. Give $\\mathrm{Sym}_p$, the space of real symmetric $p\\times p$ matrices, its Euclidean Borel sigma-algebra by identifying a matrix with its upper-triangular entries. The finite product measure $N_p(0,\\Sigma)^{\\otimes m}$ exists because each factor is a probability measure and hence sigma-finite. Define the **Wishart law** $W_p(\\Sigma,m)$ as the pushforward of this product measure by the continuous map $(z_1,\\ldots,z_m)\\mapsto\\sum_{j=1}^m z_jz_j^T$. Equivalently, it is the law of $\\sum_{j=1}^m Z_jZ_j^T$ for independent $Z_j\\sim N_p(0,\\Sigma)$.\n\nHere $\\Sigma$ is the scale matrix and the positive integer $m$ is the degrees of freedom. This definition allows a singular Wishart law when $m<p$ or $\\Sigma$ is singular; no Lebesgue density is included in the convention. The usual nonsingular density when $m\\ge p$ is not needed below.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-multivariate-normal-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC and let $d\\ge1$ be finite. For $m\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and a real symmetric positive semidefinite matrix $\\Sigma$, a Borel probability law is denoted $N_d(m,\\Sigma)$ when a vector X with that law satisfies $u\\cdot X\\sim N(u\\cdot m,u^T\\Sigma u)$ for every $u\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Such a law exists, has mean m and covariance Sigma, and can be realized as $m+\\Sigma^{1/2}Z$ with independent standard-normal coordinates. Singular Sigma is allowed. Uniqueness will be proved in the following characteristic-function lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-standard-wishart-independent-normal-quadratic-form-is-f",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $m\\ge p\\ge1$, let $W\\sim W_p(I_p,m)$, and let $Z\\sim N_p(0,I_p)$ be independent of $W$. Then $W$ is positive definite almost surely and\n$$\\frac{m-p+1}{p}Z^TW^{-1}Z\\sim F_{p,m-p+1}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-student-t-and-f-laws",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For a positive integer $k$, take the product probability law of an independent standard normal $G$ and an independent $Q_k\\sim\\chi_k^2$. The **Student t law with $k$ degrees of freedom**, denoted $t_k$, is the law of\n$$T_k=\\begin{cases} \\displaystyle\\frac{G}{\\sqrt{Q_k/k}},&Q_k>0,\\\\ 0,&Q_k\\le0. \\end{cases}$$\nFor positive integers $r,s$, take independent $Q_r\\sim\\chi_r^2$ and $Q_s\\sim\\chi_s^2$. The **Fisher F law with degrees $(r,s)$**, denoted $F_{r,s}$, is the law of\n$$F_{r,s}=\\begin{cases} \\displaystyle\\frac{Q_r/r}{Q_s/s},&Q_s>0,\\\\ 0,&Q_s\\le0. \\end{cases}$$\nThe displayed piecewise maps are defined on all of $\\mathbb R^2$ and are Borel measurable: each quotient is continuous on its Borel positive-denominator region, and the value on the complementary Borel nonpositive-denominator region is constant. The positive-degree chi-square lemma proves that the denominators are positive almost surely, so these versions agree almost surely with the usual quotients. Zero degrees are excluded from both definitions.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-chi-square-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For an integer $k\\ge1$, let $G=(G_1,\\ldots,G_k)$ have law $N_k(0,I_k)$, so its coordinates are independent $N(0,1)$ random variables. The **chi-square law with $k$ degrees of freedom**, denoted $\\chi_k^2$, is the law of\n$$\nQ_k=\\sum_{j=1}^k G_j^2=\\|G\\|^2.\n$$\nFor $k=0$, define $\\chi_0^2=\\delta_0$, the point mass at zero. This zero-degree law records a rank-zero projection only; it is not used as a denominator in a Student or Fisher ratio. The multivariate-normal characteristic-function theorem makes the definition independent of the chosen realization of $G$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-positive-df-chi-square-has-density-and-regular-cdf",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For every integer $k\\ge1$, the law $\\chi_k^2$ has density\n$$\nf_k(x)=c_k x^{k/2-1}e^{-x/2}\\mathbf 1_{\\{x>0\\}},\\qquad c_k=\\frac{\\sigma_{k-1}(S^{k-1})}{2(2\\pi)^{k/2}}>0,\n$$\nwhere $\\sigma_{k-1}$ is the polar surface measure on the unit sphere in $\\mathbb R^k$. Consequently, $Q_k>0$ almost surely, and its cumulative distribution function is continuous on $\\mathbb R$ and strictly increasing on $(0,\\infty)$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-non-negative-square-root-exists-and-is-unique",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real or complex inner product space and let\n$T:V\\to V$ be non-negative. Then there exists a unique non-negative operator\n$R:V\\to V$ such that\n\n$$R^2=T.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-measurable-functions-preserve-independence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be an independent family of random elements\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$. For each $i$, let\n$g_i:(S_i,\\Sigma_i)\\to(T_i,\\mathcal T_i)$ be measurable. Then the family\n$(g_i\\circ X_i)_{i\\in I}$ is independent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-multivariate-normal-sample-model-and-statistics",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Fix integers $n\\ge2$ and $p\\ge1$. Let $\\mathcal S_{++}^p$ be the set of real symmetric $p\\times p$ matrices $\\Sigma$ such that $v^T\\Sigma v>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^p$. The iid multivariate-normal sampling experiment has data space $\\mathcal X=(\\mathbb R^p)^n$ with sigma-algebra $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^p)^{\\otimes n}$, parameter set $\\Theta=\\mathbb R^p\\times\\mathcal S_{++}^p$, and sampling law $P_{\\mu,\\Sigma}=N_p(\\mu,\\Sigma)^{\\otimes n}$. The finite product is a probability measure by [[def-probability-measure]] and [[thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique]]. The coordinate projection $X_i$ is the complete vector observation for sampling unit $i$; its $p$ coordinates need not be independent.\n\nFor $x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\in\\mathcal X$, define\n$$\\bar x=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i,\\qquad R(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\\bar x)(x_i-\\bar x)^T,\\qquad S(x)=\\frac{R(x)}{n-1},\\qquad M(x)=\\frac{R(x)}n.$$\nThe functions $x\\mapsto\\bar x$, $S(x)$, and $M(x)$ are continuous, hence Borel measurable under AC and [[lem-ac-supplies-sequential-choices-for-probability-constructions]]; they are statistics in the experiment by [[def-statistic-and-estimator]]. Here $S$ uses the $n-1$ covariance denominator, while $M$ is the $n$-denominator covariance candidate whose maximum-likelihood property is proved separately below.\n\nThis item instantiates the mathematical portion of the scientific model contract ([[def-scientific-statistical-model-contract]]) as follows. One sampling and analysis unit is one complete observed vector $X_i$; index $i$ identifies the sampling unit and coordinate $a$ identifies a component within that vector. For fixed parameter label $\\theta=(\\mu,\\Sigma)$, $\\mu$ and $\\Sigma$ are fixed, every $X_i$ is random, every component of each $X_i$ is observed, and there are no latent quantities, predictors, treatments, or randomization. The target population for this reference experiment is the abstract law $N_p(\\mu,\\Sigma)$ of a vector-valued sampling unit, and the target parameter is $\\theta$ itself; no separate scientific estimand or physical setting is asserted. The observation map records the complete vector tuple unchanged, and inference uses the joint product law, with no conditioning convention beyond that law. No topological support or empirical adequacy claim is needed; this is a mathematical reference model, not an assertion that observed scientific data follow it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-sylvesters-criterion-for-positive-definiteness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A\\in M_n(\\mathbb R)$ be symmetric, with $n\\ge1$, and let $\\Delta_k$ be the determinant of its leading $k\\times k$ principal submatrix. Then $A$ is positive definite if and only if\n\n$$\\Delta_k>0\\qquad(1\\le k\\le n).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $n,m\\ge1$. Every continuous map\n$f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ is Borel measurable in\nthe sense of [[def-borel-and-lebesgue-measurable-function-on-rn]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "lem-ac-supplies-sequential-choices-for-probability-constructions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Then countable choice holds. Moreover, if $R\\subseteq X\\times X$ is serial on a nonempty set X and $a\\in X$, there is a sequence $(x_n)_{n\\ge0}$ with $x_0=a$ and $x_n R x_{n+1}$ for every n. Thus the countable and dependent choices required by the probability-product suppliers are available under AC.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The statistic $T^2$ in the Statement is a random variable: by [F9], $\\{S\\succ0\\}$ is Borel; on this set inversion is continuous, so the displayed quadratic form is Borel, and setting it to zero on the Borel complement preserves measurability. Let $Z=\\sqrt n\\,\\Sigma^{-1/2}(\\bar X-\\mu_0)$. The positive root is invertible by [F7]; under the null, [F1] and [F3] therefore give $Z\\sim N_p(0,I_p)$. Moreover, $Z$ and $W:=\\Sigma^{-1/2}(n-1)S\\Sigma^{-1/2}$ are independent: $\\bar X$ and $S$ are independent by [F1], and the separate whitening maps are continuous, hence Borel, so [F8] preserves independence. The congruence in [F1] and [F2] gives $W\\sim W_p(I_p,n-1)$; it is positive definite almost surely by [F1].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "On the event $S\\succ0$ (which has probability one by [F1]), substitute $S=\\Sigma^{1/2}W\\Sigma^{1/2}/(n-1)$ and $\\bar X-\\mu_0=\\Sigma^{1/2}Z/\\sqrt n$ into the definition. Cancelling the inverse congruence gives $$T^2=(n-1)Z^TW^{-1}Z.$$",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F4] with $m=n-1\\ge p$. It gives $\\frac{n-p}{p}Z^TW^{-1}Z\\sim F_{p,n-p}$. Multiplying the identity in step 2.1 by $(n-p)/(p(n-1))$ proves the asserted law. Since $S\\succ0$ almost surely, the off-domain zero convention affects only a null event.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
