    "cor-exact-hotelling-one-sample-test": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "thm-hotelling-t2-exact-f-distribution",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, $n>p\\ge1$, and the iid multivariate-normal sample model with covariance $\\Sigma\\succ0$. For $\\mu_0\\in\\mathbb R^p$, define $T^2=n(\\bar X-\\mu_0)^TS^{-1}(\\bar X-\\mu_0)$ when $S\\succ0$, and set $T^2=0$ otherwise. Under $H_0:\\mu=\\mu_0$,\n$$\\frac{n-p}{p(n-1)}T^2\\sim F_{p,n-p}.$$\nThe zero extension does not change the law because $S\\succ0$ almost surely when $n>p$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-hotelling-t2-exact-f-distribution",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, $n>p\\ge1$, and the iid multivariate-normal sample model with covariance $\\Sigma\\succ0$. For $\\mu_0\\in\\mathbb R^p$, define $T^2=n(\\bar X-\\mu_0)^TS^{-1}(\\bar X-\\mu_0)$ when $S\\succ0$, and set $T^2=0$ otherwise. Under $H_0:\\mu=\\mu_0$,\n$$\\frac{n-p}{p(n-1)}T^2\\sim F_{p,n-p}.$$\nThe zero extension does not change the law because $S\\succ0$ almost surely when $n>p$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-randomized-test",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **randomized test** is a randomized procedure with binary action space\nwhose action set is $\\{0,1\\}$, equipped with its full sigma-algebra\n$2^{\\{0,1\\}}$ (the power set, which is a sigma-algebra by\n[[def-sigma-algebra]]), where $1$ means reject and $0$ means do not reject\n([[def-randomized-procedure-as-a-markov-kernel]]). Its **test function** is\n$$\\varphi(x)=K(x,\\{1\\}),\\qquad x\\in\\mathcal X.$$\nSince $K(x,\\cdot)$ is a probability measure and $\\{0\\},\\{1\\}$ partition the\naction set, $K(x,\\{0\\})+K(x,\\{1\\})=1$. Thus $\\varphi(x)\\in[0,1]$ and\n$1-\\varphi(x)=K(x,\\{0\\})$ is the probability of not rejecting. Give $[0,1]$\nthe trace of the Borel sigma-algebra on $[0,+\\infty]$, namely\n$\\{C\\cap[0,1]:C\\in\\mathcal B([0,+\\infty])\\}$. By\n[[lem-procedure-action-law-is-measurable]], $x\\mapsto K(x,\\{1\\})$ is\nmeasurable; since it has range in $[0,1]$, its preimage of $C\\cap[0,1]$ equals\nits preimage of $C$ and is measurable for every such $C$. Hence $\\varphi$ is a\nmeasurable map in the preimage sense of\n[[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]]. Its value is the\nrejection probability after observing $x$.\nThe convention $1=\\text{reject}$ is fixed here. No null or alternative\nhypothesis is part of this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-test-size-and-power",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathsf E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$\nbe a statistical experiment ([[def-statistical-experiment]]). Let\n$\\Theta_0,\\Theta_1$ be nonempty disjoint subsets of $\\Theta$, called the null\nand alternative hypotheses. A hypothesis is **simple** when it is a singleton\nand **composite** when it has at least two elements.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-randomized-procedure-as-a-markov-kernel",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathsf E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$\nbe a statistical experiment ([[def-statistical-experiment]]) and let\n$(\\mathsf D,\\mathcal G)$ be a measurable **action space**: a set of available\nactions equipped with a sigma-algebra of action events, hence a measurable\nspace ([[def-measurable-space]]). A **randomized procedure** (or\nrandomized decision rule) is a probability kernel $K$ from\n$(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ to $(\\mathsf D,\\mathcal G)$, in the pointwise sense\nof [[def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel]]. Thus $K(x,\\cdot)$ is a\nprobability measure on $(\\mathsf D,\\mathcal G)$ for every $x\\in\\mathcal X$,\nand $x\\mapsto K(x,B)$ is $\\mathcal A$-measurable for every $B\\in\\mathcal G$.\nAfter observing $x$, the procedure assigns probability $K(x,B)$ to taking an\naction in $B$. The kernel is specified without reference to the unknown\nparameter. This definition imposes no measurable structure on $\\Theta$. The\nkernel is the specification of the procedure; no representation by a separate\nrandom seed is asserted for arbitrary measurable action spaces.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **measure kernel** from $(S,\\Sigma)$ to $(T,\\Tau)$ is a map $K:S\\times\\Tau\\to[0,\\infty]$ such that, for every $s\\in S$, $A\\mapsto K(s,A)$ is a measure on $(T,\\Tau)$, and for every $A\\in\\Tau$, $s\\mapsto K(s,A)$ is $\\Sigma$-measurable. Measures and measurability have the meanings in [[def-measure]] and [[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]]; the evaluation functions use the Borel sigma-algebra on $[0,\\infty]$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal{A})$ and $(Y,\\mathcal{B})$ be measurable spaces\n([[def-measurable-space]]). A function $f : X \\to Y$ is **measurable** when",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-student-t-and-f-cdfs-are-continuous-and-strictly-increasing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For every $k\\ge1$, the CDF of $t_k$ is continuous and strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$, is symmetric about zero, and has limits $0$ and $1$ at $-\\infty$ and $+\\infty$. For every $r,s\\ge1$, the CDF of $F_{r,s}$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R$, is zero on $(-\\infty,0]$, and is strictly increasing on $(0,\\infty)$ with limit $1$ at $+\\infty$. Consequently, for each $u\\in(0,1)$ each law has exactly one $u$-quantile on its support.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-sylvesters-criterion-for-positive-definiteness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A\\in M_n(\\mathbb R)$ be symmetric, with $n\\ge1$, and let $\\Delta_k$ be the determinant of its leading $k\\times k$ principal submatrix. Then $A$ is positive definite if and only if",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $n,m\\ge1$. Every continuous map\n$f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ is Borel measurable in\nthe sense of [[def-borel-and-lebesgue-measurable-function-on-rn]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-multivariate-normal-sample-model-and-statistics",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Fix integers $n\\ge2$ and $p\\ge1$. Let $\\mathcal S_{++}^p$ be the set of real symmetric $p\\times p$ matrices $\\Sigma$ such that $v^T\\Sigma v>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^p$. The iid multivariate-normal sampling experiment has data space $\\mathcal X=(\\mathbb R^p)^n$ with sigma-algebra $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^p)^{\\otimes n}$, parameter set $\\Theta=\\mathbb R^p\\times\\mathcal S_{++}^p$, and sampling law $P_{\\mu,\\Sigma}=N_p(\\mu,\\Sigma)^{\\otimes n}$. The finite product is a probability measure by [[def-probability-measure]] and [[thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique]]. The coordinate projection $X_i$ is the complete vector observation for sampling unit $i$; its $p$ coordinates need not be independent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The statistic is Borel measurable: the sample maps $\\bar X$ and $S$ are continuous by the model definition [F7]; by [F5], the event $S\\succ0$ is the finite intersection of strict polynomial inequalities on its leading principal minors. On this open set, $S^{-1}$ has entries equal to cofactors divided by $\\det S>0$, so the quadratic-form formula for $T^2$ is continuous by [F6]. Extending that function by zero on the Borel complement remains Borel. Let $D(x)$ be the indicator that the scaled statistic exceeds the stated threshold and define a kernel on actions $\\{0,1\\}$ by $K(x,A)=1$ if $D(x)\\in A$ and $0$ otherwise. For each $x$ this is a probability measure, and $x\\mapsto K(x,A)$ is measurable because $D$ is measurable. Thus [F3] makes this a randomized procedure and [F2] its test function.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Since $p\\ge1$, $\\mathbb R^p\\setminus\\{\\mu_0\\}$ is nonempty; hence the stated null and alternative parameter sets are nonempty and disjoint. Under AC [A1], [F4] gives a unique $q=F_{p,n-p}^{-1}(1-\\alpha)>0$ satisfying $P(F_{p,n-p}\\le q)=1-\\alpha$. For each null covariance, [F1] gives rejection probability $P(F_{p,n-p}>q)=\\alpha$, with no boundary atom because its CDF is continuous.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F7",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Taking the supremum of the same value $\\alpha$ over the nonempty null set leaves it equal to $\\alpha$. By [F2] this supremum is the test's size, proving exact size. The argument includes every positive-definite null covariance and uses the exact finite-sample F law; no asymptotic calibration is used.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
