    "ex-exact-wishart-trace-and-asymptotic-covariance-lrt-differ": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-multivariate-normal-sample-model-and-statistics",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Fix integers $n\\ge2$ and $p\\ge1$. Let $\\mathcal S_{++}^p$ be the set of real symmetric $p\\times p$ matrices $\\Sigma$ such that $v^T\\Sigma v>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^p$. The iid multivariate-normal sampling experiment has data space $\\mathcal X=(\\mathbb R^p)^n$ with sigma-algebra $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^p)^{\\otimes n}$, parameter set $\\Theta=\\mathbb R^p\\times\\mathcal S_{++}^p$, and sampling law $P_{\\mu,\\Sigma}=N_p(\\mu,\\Sigma)^{\\otimes n}$. The finite product is a probability measure by [[def-probability-measure]] and [[thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique]]. The coordinate projection $X_i$ is the complete vector observation for sampling unit $i$; its $p$ coordinates need not be independent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-covariance-likelihood-ratio-asymptotic-chi-square",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, fix $p\\ge1$, and use the iid multivariate-normal sample model with $n>p$. Test $H_0:\\Sigma=\\Sigma_0$ against unrestricted $\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal S_{++}^p$, with $\\mu\\in\\mathbb R^p$ unrestricted under both hypotheses and fixed $\\Sigma_0\\succ0$. Let $M=R/n$ be the full-model Gaussian covariance MLE, and let $\\Lambda_n$ be the null-over-full maximized likelihood ratio formed from the explicit Lebesgue Gaussian density. Almost surely,\n$$-2\\log\\Lambda_n=n\\left[\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma_0^{-1}M)-\\log\\det(\\Sigma_0^{-1/2}M\\Sigma_0^{-1/2})-p\\right].$$\nWith $p$ fixed and the null true, $-2\\log\\Lambda_n\\Rightarrow\\chi^2_{p(p+1)/2}$ as $n\\to\\infty$. The covariance null is interior in $\\mathcal S_{++}^p$. This calibration is asymptotic; no finite-sample chi-square law is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-wishart-trace-statistic-is-chi-square",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, $p\\ge1$, $n\\ge2$, and the iid multivariate-normal sample model. Under $H_0:\\Sigma=\\Sigma_0$ for a specified $\\Sigma_0\\succ0$, the distinct statistic\n$$\\operatorname{tr}\\{\\Sigma_0^{-1}(n-1)S\\}$$\nhas the exact law $\\chi^2_{p(n-1)}$, even when $n\\le p$ and $S$ is singular. This is the Wishart trace statistic, not the covariance likelihood-ratio statistic.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The sample has mean $0$, so its centered sum of squares is $R=3/2+3/2=3$. Thus $S=R/3=1$ and $M=R/4=3/4$ by [F1].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Since $p=1$ and $\\Sigma_0=1$, the trace statistic is $(n-1)S=3$, consistent with its $\\chi^2_{1\\cdot3}=\\chi^2_3$ null law [F3]. Substituting $M=3/4$ in [F2] gives the observed likelihood-ratio value $$4\\left(\\frac34-\\log\\frac34-1\\right)=4\\left(\\log\\frac43-\\frac14\\right)\\approx0.1507.$$ This is not the trace value $3$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The calculations show two different finite-sample statistics and their distinct calibrations; the fact that one observed value is $0.1507$ makes no claim about the likelihood-ratio statistic's null distribution at $n=4$. The asymptotic qualification in [F2] remains necessary.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
