          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-exact-hotelling-one-sample-test",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, $n>p\\ge1$, and the one-sample multivariate-normal model with covariance $\\Sigma\\succ0$. For a specified $\\mu_0\\in\\mathbb R^p$ and $0<\\alpha<1$, reject $H_0:\\mu=\\mu_0$ when\n$$\\frac{n-p}{p(n-1)}T^2>F_{p,n-p}^{-1}(1-\\alpha),$$\nwhere $T^2$ is the statistic in [[thm-hotelling-t2-exact-f-distribution]]. Against $H_1:\\mu\\ne\\mu_0$, this test has size exactly $\\alpha$ over all null covariances $\\Sigma\\succ0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-student-t-and-f-laws",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For a positive integer $k$, take the product probability law of an independent standard normal $G$ and an independent $Q_k\\sim\\chi_k^2$. The **Student t law with $k$ degrees of freedom**, denoted $t_k$, is the law of\n$$T_k=\\begin{cases} \\displaystyle\\frac{G}{\\sqrt{Q_k/k}},&Q_k>0,\\\\ 0,&Q_k\\le0. \\end{cases}$$\nFor positive integers $r,s$, take independent $Q_r\\sim\\chi_r^2$ and $Q_s\\sim\\chi_s^2$. The **Fisher F law with degrees $(r,s)$**, denoted $F_{r,s}$, is the law of\n$$F_{r,s}=\\begin{cases} \\displaystyle\\frac{Q_r/r}{Q_s/s},&Q_s>0,\\\\ 0,&Q_s\\le0. \\end{cases}$$\nThe displayed piecewise maps are defined on all of $\\mathbb R^2$ and are Borel measurable: each quotient is continuous on its Borel positive-denominator region, and the value on the complementary Borel nonpositive-denominator region is constant. The positive-degree chi-square lemma proves that the denominators are positive almost surely, so these versions agree almost surely with the usual quotients. Zero degrees are excluded from both definitions.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-student-t-and-f-cdfs-are-continuous-and-strictly-increasing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For every $k\\ge1$, the CDF of $t_k$ is continuous and strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$, is symmetric about zero, and has limits $0$ and $1$ at $-\\infty$ and $+\\infty$. For every $r,s\\ge1$, the CDF of $F_{r,s}$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R$, is zero on $(-\\infty,0]$, and is strictly increasing on $(0,\\infty)$ with limit $1$ at $+\\infty$. Consequently, for each $u\\in(0,1)$ each law has exactly one $u$-quantile on its support.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The four vectors sum to zero, so $\\bar x=0$. Their outer products sum to $2I_2$, hence $R=2I_2$ and $S=R/3=(2/3)I_2$ by [F1]. This matrix is positive definite and has inverse $(3/2)I_2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
